items.detail:
D1 has no interactive transactions, only batch() of statements whose results cannot feed later statements. Nearly every executor call reads, decides and writes inside one transaction:
- exec_calls dedupe;
- the proposals CAS;
- reading the chain head before an append;
- daySpent ceilings;
- lease checks;
- id_counters;
- run_taint.

It also issues hundreds of small queries per request (599 over the 67 GETs), and each would be a network round trip on D1. Keep D1 out of the data path; DO SQLite (5 GB per account on Free) is the store.

createMemoryStore (store.ts, 513 lines) implements:
- the contracts MemoryStore: init, head, read(path, at), search, write, appendTimeline, remove, tree;
- LocalMemory on top: root, chainHeadSeq, seedFiles, digest, links, aliasIndex, recall, history.

It runs git through execFile (git.ts: hardened flags, an isolated HOME in os.tmpdir, the serialGit queue, a 15 s timeout) and reads and writes the working tree with sync fs:
- search is `git grep -i --fixed-strings`;
- pinned reads are `git show <sha>:<path>`;
- history is `git log -p`;
- chainHeadSeq reads Weft-Chain-Head trailers;
- commit dates come from the sim clock, so SHAs are reproducible;
- bundle.ts and scripts/memory-bundle.ts make git bundles.

child_process is a non-functional stub on Workers, so the store fails at its first call even though it bundles cleanly. The executor builds createMemoryStore directly (executor/index.ts:77), and apps/api's memory routes, digest and recall bind to it. The seeded rich memory is small: 90 files, 30 KB of text, 36 commits.

Proposed:
- Inject `memory: (deps) => LocalMemory` into createExecutor and add a SQLite version used by both runtimes.
- Tables: memory_commits(seq, sha CHAR(40), parent, message, author, date, trailers) and memory_files(path, commit_seq, text or NULL for a delete).
- read(path, at) returns the latest version with commit_seq ≤ at's seq.
- search uses instr(lower(text), lower(term)), or FTS5 (supported in DO).
- history diffs are computed in JavaScript.
- sha is sha1 of a canonical commit preimage: 40 hex, so SHA_RE and `at` validation still pass.
- Export writes a git fast-import stream so memory:restore can rebuild a real repo.

Memory writes then become plain SQL in the caller's transaction, which also fixes git inside transactions. Run contract tests against both versions, since commit ids will differ from git's.

createScheduler ticks every 5 s with setInterval (unref'd) and on kick() after each mutation. Each tick runs, in order:
- sims.tick and rail sync;
- offer expiry;
- integration polls and webhooks;
- time-off year end and benefit windows;
- proposal expiry;
- event handlers, from an events.rowid cursor kept in settings;
- routines.tick: croner cron slots on the SimClock, then events.

The SimClock is persisted in settings: now = base_sim + (wall - base_wall) * rate, with WEFT_CLOCK_RATE defaulting to 1. One tick on the rich world ran 44 statements and about 15 ms of CPU in Node.

Hosted:
- No setInterval. DO alarms drive ticks only while a world is assigned and active, plus kick() inline after mutations. Each alarm is a DO request and each setAlarm is a row written.
- A 5 s alarm for every world all day would be 17,280 requests per world per day, against a cap of 100,000.
- Recommend clock rate 0 (frozen at the seed Monday) with the existing Simulators-panel advance buttons, or rate 1 with catch-up on the next request.
- Use Cron Triggers (5 per account) only for pool maintenance.

apps/api/src/telemetry.ts startTracingFromEnv registers @arizeai/phoenix-otel's NodeTracerProvider, with an OTLP protobuf HTTP exporter to a loopback Phoenix, only when WEFT_TRACE=phoenix. main.ts calls it; compose.ts does not. packages/agent/src/otel.ts uses only @opentelemetry/api (a no-op without a provider) and the OpenInference helpers.

Hosted: leave WEFT_TRACE unset and keep telemetry.ts out of the Worker entry. Model-call visibility comes from AI Gateway logs and Workers Logs; the in-app run trace reads run_steps.

If tracing is ever turned on hosted, three pieces of module-level state in otel.ts would leak across DO instances that share an isolate:
- the `learned` set of secrets;
- `recallNotes`, keyed by run id, and run ids restart at run_0001 in every world;
- the global setTraceRedactor.
